I'm afraid there's no way to do this without using `mouseMove` event handler. The reason being that `mouseClick` event handler is fired when the user clicks on the element. This means that the click event is already captured by the browser before it reaches your JavaScript code. So, you need to use `mouseMove` event handler to capture the movement of the mouse pointer over the image. You can then calculate the offset between the current position of the mouse pointer and the previous position of the mouse pointer. This offset can then be used to adjust the position of the image accordingly. Here's how you can do it:

```
let img = document.querySelector("img");
let prevMouseX = null;
let prevMouseY = null;

img.addEventListener("mousemove", function(event){
    let mouseX = event.clientX;
    let mouseY = event.clientY;

    if(prevMouseX !== null && prevMouseY !== null){
        let offsetX = mouseX - prevMouseX;
        let offsetY = mouseY - prevMouseY;

        img.style.left = parseInt(img.style.left || 0) + offsetX + "px";
        img.style.top = parseInt(img.style.top || 0) + offsetY + "px";
    }

    prevMouseX = mouseX;
    prevMouseY = mouseY;
});
```

I'm not sure what you're asking. You can't use `torch.Tensor` objects directly in C++ code. If you want to do computations in C++, you need to convert your tensors into raw data structures like `std::vector`. For example:

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <torch/torch.h>

int main() {
  auto tensor = torch::randn({2, 3});
  std::cout << tensor << "\n";

  // Convert the tensor to a vector.
  std::vector<float> vec(tensor.data_ptr<float>(), tensor.data_ptr<float>() + tensor.numel());

  // Do some computations on the vector.
  for (auto& elem : vec) {
    elem *= 2;
  }

  // Convert the vector back to a tensor.
  auto result = torch::from_blob(vec.data(), {2, 3}, at::kFloat);
  std::cout << result << "\n";
}
```

